 * `[SPN-1]` `Array[T]` coerces to `Span[T]` at borrow sites and to `MutSpan[T]` at `mut` sites; `[T; N]` likewise; `String` to `str`. **This coercion takes a borrow of the source; it is not a conversion.** Coercing to `Span[T]` creates a live shared borrow of the source place and coercing to `MutSpan[T]` a mutable one, and the resulting view's region is that borrow's, under the ordinary rules of `[BRW-1]` and `[BRW-2]` — so the source may not be mutated, moved or dropped while the view is live, and the borrow ends at the view's last use and not at the end of scope. The same holds however the view is spelled: the implicit coercion, `as_span()`/`as_mut_span()`, and a view returned from a call under `[LT-1]`'s elision are one construction, and all three take the borrow. An implementation MUST make this borrow explicit in its IR rather than implied by the coercion, because an implied borrow is invisible to the borrow checker: `v: Span[i32] = a` followed by `a.push(...)` is then accepted, the push reallocates, and safe code reads freed memory. *(clarified 2026-09-09; see `docs/spec-amendments.md`)*
 * `[SPN-2]` Indexing a `Span` is bounds-checked; `get(i) -> Option[ref T]` is the checked-without-panic form; `unsafe: s.get_unchecked(i)`.
 * `[SPN-3]` `Span[T]` is `Copy`; `MutSpan[T]` is move-only and reborrowable (`s.reborrow()` or implicit at `mut` sites).
+* `[SPN-4]` **Canonical iterator types.** `std.collections` publicly exports
+  `SpanIter[T]`, `MutSpanIter[T]`, `SpanChunks[T]`, and `MutSpanChunks[T]`.
+  None is a prelude name. They are named `@view` iterator types implementing
+  the existing associated-type interface as follows:
+
+  ```ember
+  SpanIter[T]       implements Iterator[Item = ref T]
+  MutSpanIter[T]    implements Iterator[Item = ref mut T]
+  SpanChunks[T]     implements Iterator[Item = Span[T]]
+  MutSpanChunks[T]  implements Iterator[Item = MutSpan[T]]
+  ```
+
+  These are ordinary library types, not compiler intrinsics or new ownership
+  categories. Their concrete names make the associated `Item` contracts
+  expressible without introducing opaque iterator returns.
+* `[SPN-5]` **Iterator construction.** The canonical APIs are given by this
+  standard-library signature sketch:
+
+  ```ember,ignore
+  Span.iter(self) -> SpanIter[T]
+  MutSpan.iter(self) -> SpanIter[T]
+  MutSpan.iter_mut(mut self) -> MutSpanIter[T]
+  ```
+
+  `MutSpan.iter()` creates a shared reborrow and prevents conflicting mutation
+  for the resulting iterator's lifetime. `MutSpan.iter_mut()` creates a
+  mutable reborrow and does not consume the underlying `MutSpan`; after that
+  iterator's borrow ends, ordinary NLL/reborrow rules permit the original
+  `MutSpan` to be used again. No iterator may outlive its source view.
+* `[SPN-6]` **Chunk APIs.** The canonical APIs are given by this
+  standard-library signature sketch:
+
+  ```ember,ignore
+  Span.chunks(self, n: usize) -> SpanChunks[T]
+  MutSpan.chunks(self, n: usize) -> SpanChunks[T]
+  MutSpan.chunks_mut(mut self, n: usize) -> MutSpanChunks[T]
+  ```
+
+  For `n > 0`, iteration partitions the source into consecutive,
+  non-overlapping chunks `[0 .. n]`, `[n .. 2n]`, and so on through the
+  source length. The final chunk MAY contain fewer than `n` elements.
+  `chunks_mut` produces disjoint `MutSpan[T]` values whose storage identities
+  cover only their respective ranges; no two yielded mutable chunks may
+  overlap. It is a sanctioned construction under `[BRW-5]`, not a new
+  aliasing mechanism.
+* `[SPN-7]` **Zero chunk size.** `chunks(0)` and `chunks_mut(0)` are invalid and
+  MUST panic through the existing bounds/argument failure mechanism in every
+  supported profile. They MUST NOT return an empty iterator or `Result`, yield
+  zero-width chunks, or loop indefinitely. A future change to this failure
+  policy requires an explicit language revision.
+* `[SPN-8]` **Raw-pointer extraction.** The canonical APIs are given by this
+  standard-library signature sketch:
+
+  ```ember,ignore
+  Span.as_ptr(self) -> *T
+  MutSpan.as_ptr(self) -> *T
+  MutSpan.as_mut_ptr(mut self) -> *mut T
+  ```
+
+  Extraction itself is safe because it neither dereferences nor accesses the
+  pointed-to memory. Dereference, reads, writes, reference construction, and
+  pointer arithmetic not otherwise proven safe require the existing `unsafe`
+  rules. `MutSpan.as_mut_ptr` takes a mutable reborrow and does not consume the
+  underlying view. A returned pointer carries no safe Ember borrow guarantee.
+* `[SPN-9]` **Pointer lifetime and provenance.** `as_ptr` and `as_mut_ptr` do
+  not extend, retain, or pin the source view or its storage. Code leaving the
+  safe borrow model through a raw pointer is responsible for validity under
+  the existing unsafe contract.
+* `[SPN-10]` **Public module boundary.** The four iterator/chunk types are
+  public exports of `std.collections`, but none is exported by the prelude.
+  No opaque iterator-return mechanism is introduced by this completion.
+* `[TST-25]` **Span API conformance.** Conformance MUST cover: (1)
+  `Span.iter()` yielding `ref T`; (2) `MutSpan.iter()` yielding shared `ref T`;
+  (3) `MutSpan.iter_mut()` yielding `ref mut T`; (4) reuse of the original
+  `MutSpan` after the mutable iterator borrow ends; (5) rejection of conflicting
+  access while an iterator/reborrow remains live; (6) normal and final-partial
+  `chunks(n)`; (7) non-overlapping mutable results from `chunks_mut(n)`; (8)
+  mutable-chunk alias rejection and interaction with `split_at_mut`; (9)
+  all-profile panic through the existing failure path for `chunks(0)` and
+  `chunks_mut(0)`; (10) safe extraction through `as_ptr` and `as_mut_ptr`; (11)
+  unsafe-only dereference/use of raw pointers; (12) no source-lifetime extension
+  from pointer extraction; (13) generic `T` substitution through every iterator
+  and chunk type; and (14) ABI/layout erasure of region metadata. This matrix
+  introduces no new borrowing or lifetime mechanism.
 
